{
  "channels": {
    "_comment": "One member's overlay, deep-merged over config.json by entrypoint-multiuser.sh (NANOBOT_INSTANCE=userN). It exists for one reason: WhatsApp is linked to ONE person's account, so exactly one of the five instances may run that channel. The other four have no overlay and never dial a bridge. When another person is linked, they get their own file plus their own whatsapp-bridge-userN service — not an edit to the shared config. NOTE: this comment sits inside `channels` and not at the top level on purpose — Config forbids unknown keys there, so a _comment one level up does not fail this file, it fails the container, for all five of them at once.",
    "whatsapp": {
      "_comment_enabled": "On. This is that member's own account and the point is that Alfred reads it, so there is nothing here to withhold until somebody is enumerated. `enabled` and `allowFrom` must still move together: an enabled channel with an EMPTY allowFrom is not a closed default, it makes nanobot exit at startup — that restart-looped this instance 74 times on 2026-08-16 and that member had no Alfred at all until it was found.",
      "enabled": true,
      "_comment_bridgeUrl": "The service name, because the bridge is its own container on the compose network. It used to be 127.0.0.1 with the two sharing a network namespace, which kept the bridge on loopback and cost more than it saved: a joined namespace dies with its owner, so every restart of this container left the bridge unable to resolve anything (see docker-compose.multiuser.yml).",
      "bridgeUrl": "ws://whatsapp-bridge-userN:3002",
      "_comment_bridgeToken": "Deliberately absent. An empty bridgeToken sends the channel to _load_or_create_bridge_token, which reads or creates AUTH_DIR/bridge-token — the same file bridge/src/index.ts resolves — so the two agree over the volume they already share and the secret never exists outside the deployment. Naming the WHATSAPP_BRIDGE_TOKEN env var here would be worse than redundant: config/loader.py interpolates env references in EVERY string, comments included, and raises when one is unset — so it would turn a deleted credential into a container that never starts. For the same reason this sentence spells the variable out rather than writing it as a reference.",
      "_comment_allowFrom": "\"*\" on purpose, and it is not the open door it looks like. It gates exactly one thing: whether a message may become a TURN. It never gated reading — every message is stored either way — and keeping a list of every contact and group member who might ever type Alfred's name is a chore whose failure mode is the outage above. Three other things do the actual protecting, and all of them stay. addressTrigger: only a message that says his name becomes a turn, so the family group talking amongst itself is stored and passed over in silence. reply_mode, per chat, starting at 'off': a stranger gets silence no matter what they write, because nothing is sent until the member switches that conversation on. And is_third_party_session in the runner, which holds these turns to _ASK_READABLE_ACTIONS — household reads like chores and the menu, never a write, and deliberately never the whatsapp or notifications reads, so nobody can ask Alfred what is in their chats. What \"*\" does cost: a stranger who writes \"alfred ...\" to this number spends a turn. If that ever becomes spam or a bill, this list is the lever to reach for.",
      "allowFrom": ["*"],
      "_comment_addressTrigger": "Alfred answers only a message that says his name — 'alfred, ¿a qué hora...?'. Everything else is stored and passed over in silence. Empty would restore upstream's behaviour of answering every allowed message, which on a real person's WhatsApp means replying to the family all day.",
      "addressTrigger": "alfred",
      "_comment_groupPolicy": "'open' because addressTrigger is already the gate and it is the better one: it works the same in a group and in a one-to-one, and it does not depend on anyone knowing to @mention a contact that is really a bot. 'mention' would additionally require a WhatsApp @-mention of the member's own number, which is not what people type when they want the assistant.",
      "groupPolicy": "open"
    }
  }
}
